Vendor: Juniper | Region: National Core | Target: MME (Mobility Management Entity) | Steps: Step 1: Verify MME process health and resource utilization. High CPU or memory can cause silent drops. Command: top -c 1 | grep mme Step 2: Check for dropped packets at the ingress interface level. Focus on interfaces handling UE traffic. Command: show interfaces terse | match 'ge-0/0/0' | match 'drops' Step 3: Temporarily disable rate-limiting on the suspected ingress interface to determine if it's contributing to the drops. **WARNING: This can overload downstream resources. Monitor closely.** Command: configure
set interfaces ge-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet filter input {term 1 from address-prefix 0.0.0.0/0 then discard}
delete interfaces ge-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet filter input term 1
commit Step 4: Revert the rate-limiting change if it did not resolve the issue. This is critical to prevent potential network overload. Command: configure
set interfaces ge-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet filter input {term 1 from address-prefix 0.0.0.0/0 then discard}
commit Step 5: Examine the MME's control plane logs for errors related to UE attach procedures or signaling. Look for messages indicating resource allocation failures. Command: show log messages | match 'mme' | match 'attach' Step 6: If the issue persists after steps 1-5, escalate to the Juniper TAC with the collected logs, configuration snippets, and observed behavior. Provide details of the attempted mitigation steps. Command: N/A - Escalation to TAC
